<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
  >

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

  <!--
    Predictive back is opted out of, which for targetSdk 16+ means opting out of a platform default.

    Declaring a shared element across the two destinations (the family name, which grows from a
    gallery row into the specimen headline) keeps the outgoing screen's predictive-back preview
    alive past the pop: the specimen shrinks, hangs over the restored gallery for ~150ms, then
    disappears. Measured at ~500ms against ~317ms for button back.

    Isolated to the shared element itself: SharedTransitionLayout alone is clean, and so is the
    same NavHost with no sharedBounds declared. It is not the bounds spring, the content
    enter/exit, or SharedContentConfig's permitTransformDuringDeferredTransition /
    shouldKeepEnabledForOngoingAnimation. All four were tried; none of them reach it.

    The documented pattern is the one used here (SharedTransitionLayout around NavHost, Navigation
    2.10.0), and the docs list no known issue. The APIs aimed at this are new in the August '26
    Compose release and still carry an open bug, b/342204665.

    So this is a temporary measure, in the sense the docs use the word. Revisit with Navigation 3,
    where predictive back is supported natively.
  -->
  <application
    android:name=".App"
    android:allowBackup="true"
    android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="false"
    android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
    android:label="@string/app_name"
    android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
    android:supportsRtl="true"
    android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
    tools:targetApi="37"
    >

    <activity
      android:name=".MainActivity"
      android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|keyboardHidden"
      android:exported="true"
      >
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

  </application>

</manifest>
